        "fr-extract.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "ca85614a-b541-3fd4-be4d-fb5f433b7b93",
                "word": "monosème",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "bf84bce5-d263-41c9-bb4a-0c1cbdc0cee9",
                        "entry_id": "ca85614a-b541-3fd4-be4d-fb5f433b7b93",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural",
                            "masculine",
                            "feminine"
                        ],
                        "form": "monosèmes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4ba00354-31c2-378b-84f5-a01349e7d0a7",
                        "entry_id": "ca85614a-b541-3fd4-be4d-fb5f433b7b93",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Qui n'a qu'une seule signification."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "236925c9-4b15-4be3-9a89-bfc6eff5723c",
                                "sense_id": "4ba00354-31c2-378b-84f5-a01349e7d0a7",
                                "text": "La substitution vocable monosème / vocable polysème."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bdcb46c4-b6dc-44aa-aa12-b1ae1a0b71a6",
                                "sense_id": "4ba00354-31c2-378b-84f5-a01349e7d0a7",
                                "text": "Il est conseiller de créer autant de dictionnaires qu'il y a de thèmes traités, et non de faire un énorme dictionnaire, et que chaque entrée (mot) soit monosème (le mot n'a qu'un seul sens) et non-redondant (éviter les synonymes, sauf contextuels);"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "35c4b20d-c9a9-4f14-a9e9-cc669081a541",
                        "source_entry_id": "ca85614a-b541-3fd4-be4d-fb5f433b7b93",
                        "source_sense_id": "4ba00354-31c2-378b-84f5-a01349e7d0a7",
                        "target_lang_word": "monoseme",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "monoseme",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "57dfdaab-84c8-38f2-9b11-16950981477a",
                                        "entry_id": "40e4993b-bb31-3b14-9080-f8b5ec2ae9c0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A single semeion or mora."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "426981e9-eaab-4e14-a797-f028d819e3da",
                                                "sense_id": "57dfdaab-84c8-38f2-9b11-16950981477a",
                                                "text": "The foot pattern for the baby caprine song is: diseme/monoseme/diseme/diseme."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7b27914b-2c97-4383-84c3-36bb4d460b88",
                                                "sense_id": "57dfdaab-84c8-38f2-9b11-16950981477a",
                                                "text": "In a given poem, a monoseme contains a short vowel of one mora, and a diseme contains two moras."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5fd77569-ec23-427a-b94e-466645fcd96f",
                                                "sense_id": "57dfdaab-84c8-38f2-9b11-16950981477a",
                                                "text": "But its primary meaning must be rhythmic, since it is not used to group notes longer than a monoseme […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f1c945b8-c8ee-3f8e-a220-e3a6124ccc5e",
                                        "entry_id": "40e4993b-bb31-3b14-9080-f8b5ec2ae9c0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A monosemous term; a term with one meaning."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9ff53e93-fe19-46ec-b517-11fe6784e88e",
                                                "sense_id": "f1c945b8-c8ee-3f8e-a220-e3a6124ccc5e",
                                                "text": "Moreover, a word that is a monoseme in general language can be used equivocally in a special language."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "eef8592c-3d3a-452f-a79d-53a8770bef30",
                                                "sense_id": "f1c945b8-c8ee-3f8e-a220-e3a6124ccc5e",
                                                "text": "As a dictionary search makes clear, preciously few ordinary words are monosemes, but they do exist: hippopotamus may be an example."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fd6989cd-562f-414d-8ce6-9799fc8b5dce",
                                                "sense_id": "f1c945b8-c8ee-3f8e-a220-e3a6124ccc5e",
                                                "text": "The simplest kind of word is a monoseme […] So many words develop additional meanings over time that it’s quite difficult to think of examples, but three possibilities are telephone, word-processor, giraffe – all fairly recent additions to the English vocabulary."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6f107838-973f-32ed-a9f4-3b96a4bbef29",
                                        "entry_id": "40e4993b-bb31-3b14-9080-f8b5ec2ae9c0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An underlying meaning of a word, more general than a sememe."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "43ba2d7e-e002-49c8-9f74-2887b378bf3d",
                                                "sense_id": "6f107838-973f-32ed-a9f4-3b96a4bbef29",
                                                "text": "A monoseme in the semantic respect is less than a word but in the syntactical configuration — more."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ee00f1ec-44fd-49d2-aecd-b3b06f6538a9",
                                                "sense_id": "6f107838-973f-32ed-a9f4-3b96a4bbef29",
                                                "text": "Our observations made it clear that the new meanings, which emerged in the inner forms of the lexical units, had taken root under direct “pressure” of the monosemes of the confronted and identified counterpart in the source-language."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5b9663d6-7bad-44d7-bf31-6c34f84e169f",
                                                "sense_id": "6f107838-973f-32ed-a9f4-3b96a4bbef29",
                                                "text": "Thus, we suggest that in semantic structures of standard words there emerge nonstandard monosemes which are stylistically marked or socially determined and dependent on contexts and extra-linguistic situations."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "352712a3-29e6-48b5-9a16-082a7f63b548",
                                                "sense_id": "6f107838-973f-32ed-a9f4-3b96a4bbef29",
                                                "text": "In many modern studies the underlying meaning of a preposition such as over is seen as a group of related meanings, a sort of molecule. Others see it as a single meaning, a monoseme, or ideal meaning, Jakobson’s Gesamtbedeutung […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "monoseme",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3c8964f9-eaf3-3f35-8fb1-e718cc4c485c",
                                        "entry_id": "ceb23d70-7da8-369a-bfba-fbde604bc642",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Monosemic."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable",
                                            "rare"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b87e271f-63d2-4c63-bbcf-2303e2ebaefe",
                                                "sense_id": "3c8964f9-eaf3-3f35-8fb1-e718cc4c485c",
                                                "text": "Thus, in the early period of PIE lexical units having conglomerate monoseme internal forms with “existential” (motional), “spatial”, and “temporal” meanings began to polarize."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "668757e8-bfbd-4b39-bcbd-473c1b35cc7b",
                                                "sense_id": "3c8964f9-eaf3-3f35-8fb1-e718cc4c485c",
                                                "text": "The insensibility towards a semantic structure adaptation goes so far that the (also imperfective) monoseme synonym jijam of grem in the meaning ‘to go’ is disappearing nowadays."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1763d016-7bc8-48c9-8d0a-31e016dd5d3c",
                                                "sense_id": "3c8964f9-eaf3-3f35-8fb1-e718cc4c485c",
                                                "text": "The Dutch word ‘loods’, as a person, is monoseme and can only mean ‘a pilot in shipping’, and certainly not ‘airplane pilot’ or any other type of guide."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
                "entry_id": "d2b12ba7-b983-366a-ad71-d5c5868a9512",
                "word": "monosème",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "dbd1dc9a-7826-417c-8837-8224da40e998",
                        "entry_id": "d2b12ba7-b983-366a-ad71-d5c5868a9512",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "monosèmes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d1e9209f-ccb3-3e0c-973f-c679b42064df",
                        "entry_id": "d2b12ba7-b983-366a-ad71-d5c5868a9512",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Mot n'ayant qu'une seule signification."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "b74af9b9-c6cb-4dff-a506-193efae09c0d",
                                "sense_id": "d1e9209f-ccb3-3e0c-973f-c679b42064df",
                                "text": "Un monosème, ou un mot monosémique, est un signe linguistique dont le sémantisme plénier est réalisé du premier coup"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0ae5a764-1e86-4f36-97b1-e0672054b369",
                                "sense_id": "d1e9209f-ccb3-3e0c-973f-c679b42064df",
                                "text": "Un item lexical est une unité de sens ; c'est un monosème, selon un certain angle de vue qui demande à être défini."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "a73a3870-d738-4cd8-9dd7-ba93099ee99b",
                        "source_entry_id": "d2b12ba7-b983-366a-ad71-d5c5868a9512",
                        "source_sense_id": "d1e9209f-ccb3-3e0c-973f-c679b42064df",
